{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792946892,"nonce":"WHmTAc3rC46dvRMsNxM9YPeKN5g=","title":"Snapshots carry the runtime library as machine code: built at build time for every target, and CI-served LLVM builds","message":"The builtins snapshot carries the library's declarations and HIR, so every process lowers, stubs and compiles the library again: zylua's empty script adopts 133 library functions and makes 71 lazy stubs before it runs (about 1.5 of its 8 ms on the NUC, b399633), and zypy's deltablue compiles 65 library functions (70 ms of CPU) in its first iteration (c9a23f1). The user's direction: snapshots are compiled ahead of time, served through the zyntax distribution via CI, and available on every supported platform; everything ships in one executable.\n\nPlatforms: x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu, x86_64-pc-windows-msvc, x86_64-apple-darwin (CI's native matrix), aarch64-apple-darwin (benchmarks, development). wasm32 keeps a HIR-only snapshot.\n\nDelivery, both:\n1. Build time. Each frontend's build.rs already lowers the library for the target; it also compiles it with Cranelift to an object for the target triple (ELF, Mach-O, COFF) and links it into the executable. Every target cargo builds gets one; CI's matrix tests all three formats.\n2. CI-served. A release workflow builds LLVM-optimised snapshot objects per target, keyed by the compiler build fingerprint (ZYNTAX_COMPILER_BUILD_ID) and target triple, and publishes them; build.rs uses one when its key matches and falls back to the build-time Cranelift snapshot otherwise (development builds, offline builds).\n\nWhat the code generator has to change: the Cranelift backend writes run-time addresses into code as constants, which an object cannot hold:\n- call cells (reload::call_cell_addr) at direct calls and function values;\n- runtime symbol pointers at calls into the runtime and plugins;\n- OSR probe slots and entry counters (osr::helper_slot_addr, entry_counter_addr) keyed by beads the runtime assigns;\n- the stub's published-entry slot.\nAll of these go through one emitter that writes the constant for the JIT and a relocation against a named symbol for an object; the snapshot defines the cells, bead slots and counters as its own data, and the loader binds them. Library functions call each other through cells, so promoting one to LLVM at run time still takes effect, and their HIR stays in the snapshot for inlining and for resume points (compiled from the same body, so site keys agree).\n\nPhases:\n1. Address emitter in cranelift_backend.rs (JIT behaviour unchanged).\n2. Object output: the backend over cranelift-object's ObjectModule for a given triple; data symbols for cells, bead slots and counters.\n3. build.rs emits and links the object; the snapshot records each function's symbol.\n4. Loader: library functions start compiled (bead installed, cell set to the snapshot's entry), no stubs, no adoption until a body is needed.\n5. CI release workflow and the matching fetch in build.rs.\n\nMeasure each phase on zylua's empty script and deltablue's first iteration (NUC, interleaved).","files":null}]}